    "summary": "A world-famous, spectacular residential neighborhood display of over-the-top Christmas lights, giant inflatables, and massive holiday decorations.",
    "desc": "The Exact Coordinates\nDo not wander aimlessly. While your GPS might point to the outskirts, the absolute densest, most spectacular concentration of lights sits firmly between 83rd and 86th Streets, stretching from 11th to 13th Avenues.\n\nThe Free Attraction Hack\nEmbrace the massive value of this community tradition. You do not need to book expensive, commercial bus tours, as walking the brilliantly illuminated neighborhood grid is completely free for all visitors.\n\nThe Weekday Strategy\nYou absolutely must plan your visit for a Monday through Thursday evening during the first two weeks of December. This highly strategic timing completely bypasses the massive, shoulder-to-shoulder weekend tourist crowds.\n\nThe Sunset Arrival\nTime your transit perfectly to arrive just as the sun dips below the horizon around 4:30 PM. Watching the quiet residential grid suddenly ignite with millions of glowing bulbs is a brilliant, highly cinematic experience.\n\nThe Spata House\nSeek out 1152 84th Street to witness the historic epicenter of the tradition. This incredibly dense, overwhelming visual masterpiece is completely blanketed in bright lights and serves as one of the most famous stops on the route.\n\nThe Polizzotto House\nWalk directly across the street to 1145 84th Street for a completely different scale of holiday engineering. Admire the incredibly massive, highly motorized giant Santa display that is renowned throughout the entire neighborhood.\n\nThe Curfew Warning\nDo not push your arrival late into the evening. Because these are private homes, many residents manually power down their massive, highly engineered displays right around 9:00 PM to conserve energy and reduce neighborhood noise.\n\nThe Restroom Reality\nExecute absolute precision with your fluid intake before arriving at the neighborhood borders. Because this is a strictly residential zone, there are absolutely zero public restrooms available anywhere along the main walking routes.\n\nThe Transit Evasion\nNever attempt to drive a vehicle into the glowing grid. The narrow residential streets become completely gridlocked with massive tour buses and pedestrians, making it incredibly stressful and nearly impossible to secure parking.\n\nThe D Train Approach\nNavigate to the spectacle flawlessly by riding the D train directly to the 79th Street and New Utrecht Avenue station. Emerging from the underground leaves you with a highly scenic, brisk fifteen-minute urban walk straight into the glowing heart of the displays.\n\nThe Pacing Protocol\nAllocate exactly one to two hours for a complete, highly satisfying neighborhood expedition. This provides the absolute perfect amount of time to seamlessly walk every single major block without feeling rushed by the dropping temperatures.\n\nThe Sidewalk Boundary\nExercise immense respect for the local architecture and landscaping. Keep your entire group strictly on the paved sidewalks, as stepping onto the highly decorated lawns or driveways can easily damage the delicate, complex displays.\n\nThe Exposure Adjustment\nBefore taking your first photograph, manually tap your smartphone screen and heavily drag the exposure slider down. This brilliant technical adjustment prevents the incredibly bright LEDs from blowing out the image, preserving the rich, vibrant colors. \n\nThe Wide-Angle Capture\nSwitch your smartphone camera to its dedicated 0.5x ultra-wide lens to perfectly capture the sprawling properties. A sweeping shot flawlessly frames the massive, glowing two-story mansions and their densely packed front yards in one brilliant image. \n\nThe 13th Avenue Pivot\nOnce you complete your loop and the freezing temperatures set in, take a highly strategic walk toward 13th Avenue. Transition seamlessly into this bustling commercial strip to secure a steaming hot chocolate from local vendors to warm up before the subway ride home.",
