    "address": "Dalmatian and Taxi Sculpture \"Spot\", 424 E 34th St, New York, NY 10016, United States",
    "summary": "A whimsical, 38-foot-tall public sculpture of a giant Dalmatian balancing a real NYC taxi on its nose outside a children's hospital.",
    "desc": "The Exact Coordinates\nDo not wander aimlessly along the East River. This massive, highly whimsical public art installation is brilliantly anchored directly outside the Hassenfeld Children's Hospital entrance at 424 East 34th Street. \n\nThe Free Attraction Hack\nEmbrace the incredible value of world-class urban street art. Viewing, photographing, and interacting with this towering, heavily engineered masterpiece is completely free and accessible to the public twenty-four hours a day.\n\nThe Architectural Scale\nPrepare to be completely overwhelmed by the sheer magnitude of the piece. The incredibly massive fiberglass Dalmatian towers an astonishing thirty-eight feet into the air, easily dwarfing the surrounding pedestrian plaza.\n\nThe Prius Payload\nLook closely at the bright yellow taxi precariously balanced on the dog's nose. It is not a hollow replica, but an actual, deeply authentic Toyota Prius completely stripped of its engine and heavy transmission.\n\nThe Weather Effects\nIf you happen to visit during a sudden New York downpour, watch the car closely. The artist flawlessly engineered the vehicle to have fully functional windshield wipers that activate when it rains.\n\nThe Night Illumination\nPlan your visit just after sundown for a brilliantly dramatic visual shift. The heavy headlights of the taxi are completely operational and brightly illuminate the surrounding medical campus at night.\n\nThe Healing Mission\nAppreciate the deeply emotional context anchoring the whimsical installation. The artist brilliantly designed the heroic, playful animal doing the impossible specifically to uplift and comfort the young children undergoing treatment inside the hospital.\n\nThe Ferry Approach\nDitch the crowded subway system for a highly scenic, incredibly relaxing arrival. Take the NYC Ferry directly to the East 34th Street terminal, leaving you with an effortless, brilliant four-minute walk straight to the sculpture.\n\nThe East River Pivot\nOnce you secure your massive photographs, take a highly strategic walk straight toward the water. Transition seamlessly into the East River Esplanade to enjoy a brilliant, highly scenic waterfront stroll with sweeping views of Queens.\n\nThe Upward Angle\nTo capture the absolute best social media image, drop incredibly low to the pavement. Shooting sharply upward brilliantly frames the massive dog against the towering, sleek glass facade of the modern hospital behind it.\n\nThe Subway Reality\nIf you do utilize the subway, prepare for a moderate urban trek. Riding the 6 train to the 33rd Street station requires a brisk, highly energetic walk east across several massive, bustling city avenues.\n\nThe Macro Detail\nActivate your camera's dedicated macro mode before stepping away from the base. Get incredibly close to beautifully capture the flawless, heavy fiberglass texture of the black spots wrapping around the massive dog's paws.\n\nThe Traffic Warning\nExercise massive caution when stepping backward to fit the towering piece into your camera frame. The surrounding 34th Street corridor operates as a highly congested, rapid-fire traffic artery for the medical center and FDR Drive.\n\nThe Time Lapse Capture\nFor a brilliant, cinematic video clip, utilize your smartphone's built-in time-lapse feature. Set up across the street to flawlessly capture the massive dog standing frozen while heavy NYC traffic and pedestrians blur rapidly in the foreground.\n\nThe St. Vartan Escape\nIf you need a quiet moment after navigating the bustling medical district, head directly west. Walk a few short blocks to seamlessly transition into St. Vartan Park for a beautifully shaded, deeply relaxing neighborhood sanctuary.",
